          <h2>Understanding Digital File Compression &amp; Target Constraints</h2>
          <p>
            Uploading digital documents to official portals often results in unexpected rejections. Most recruitment
            databases, admission gates, and government platforms configure server-side validation filters with strict
            upper and lower limits on file size, pixel dimensions, and resolution metadata. CONVERTkb was built to solve
            this exact friction point directly inside your web browser.
          </p>

          <h3>Why Exact KB Constraints Matter for Application Portals</h3>
          <p>
            Public service portals handle millions of applications within narrow application windows. To conserve database
            throughput and guarantee uniform printing of identity badges, hall tickets, and application forms, system
            architects set strict boundaries. A portal may mandate that an applicant photo must be between 20KB and 50KB,
            formatted at 200 DPI, and measured exactly to 3.5 cm x 4.5 cm.
          </p>

          <h3>Client-Side Processing: Genuine Privacy by Architecture</h3>
          <p>
            Unlike legacy conversion sites that require uploading personal identity documents, signatures, and certificates to
            remote servers, CONVERTkb processes every byte locally on your machine. That means your data never leaves your
            device, and the browser performs the full conversion in real time.
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li><strong>Zero Data Transmission:</strong> Your image data is decoded inside a browser canvas on your computer or phone.</li>
            <li><strong>Local Re-encoding:</strong> The canvas output is saved to a blob using native browser APIs.</li>
            <li><strong>Instant Execution:</strong> No upload/download latency means the whole process completes in milliseconds.</li>
          </ul>

          <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

          <div class="faq-item">
            <div class="faq-question">How do I resize an image to 50KB without losing clarity?</div>
            <div class="faq-answer">Use precise optimization rather than broad percentage reduction. Keep the natural pixel dimensions while removing extra metadata and balancing quality settings.</div>
          </div>

          <div class="faq-item">
            <div class="faq-question">What is DPI and why do portals ask for 200 or 300 DPI?</div>
            <div class="faq-answer">DPI stands for Dots Per Inch, a printer density measure. Many government and exam portals require fixed DPI values so printed documents are readable and consistent.</div>
          </div>

          <div class="faq-item">
            <div class="faq-question">Can I convert iPhone HEIC photos to JPG under 50KB?</div>
            <div class="faq-answer">Yes. Modern Apple devices often capture HEIC files, which many portals reject. Convert them locally to JPG in compressed form without uploading personal images anywhere.</div>
          </div>
        </div>
